 # Exact Visibility Ordering for Cell Projection Volume Rendering (SXMPVO)
 
-Also called: Image-space visibility ordering for cell projection volume rendering, SXMPVO (Scan-conversion based MPVO), SXMPVO algorithm, Scalable Multi-Processor Volume Ordering, Scanline-based MPVO, Scanning Exact Meshed Polyhedra Visibilit, Scanning Exact Meshed Polyhedra Visibility Ordering, ZSWEEP
+Also called: Scanning Exact Meshed Polyhedra Visibility Ordering
 
 ## Overview
-SXMPVO is an algorithm for determining the visibility ordering of cells in nonconvex unstructured meshes for volume rendering [S2, S3]. It solves the problem that the standard Maximal Polyhedra Visibility Ordering (MPVO) method fails for nonconvex meshes because it ignores ordering relations along viewing rays that cross empty space ("ray-gaps") between non-adjacent cells [S2]. SXMPVO achieves "image-space correctness" by combining MPVO's face-based partial ordering with scan-conversion of exterior faces into an A-Buffer to resolve these ray-gaps [S2, S3]. Unlike object-precision methods like XMPVO, SXMPVO uses image-precision ray shooting, making it faster and simpler to implement while guaranteeing correct sorting for acyclic meshes [S2, S3].
+SXMPVO is an algorithm for determining the visibility ordering of cells in nonconvex unstructured meshes for volume rendering [S2, S3, unofficial]. It solves the problem that the standard Maximal Polyhedra Visibility Ordering (MPVO) method fails for nonconvex meshes because it ignores ordering relations along viewing rays that cross empty space ("ray-gaps") between non-adjacent cells [S2, unofficial]. SXMPVO achieves "image-space correctness" by combining MPVO's face-based partial ordering with scan-conversion of exterior faces into an A-Buffer to resolve these ray-gaps [S2, S3, unofficial]. Unlike object-precision methods like XMPVO, SXMPVO uses image-precision ray shooting, making it faster and simpler to implement while guaranteeing correct sorting for acyclic meshes [S2, S3, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
-The algorithm computes a global ordering of cells in an unstructured mesh (tetrahedral, hexahedral, or "zoo" meshes) to produce correct segment sorting along viewing rays [S2, S3]. It assumes the mesh is acyclic; if a cycle exists, the algorithm reports it [S2, S3]. The mesh may have nonconvex boundaries, disconnected components, and cells with nonplanar surfaces, provided the projections of cell edges do not cross ("bow tie" quadrilaterals) [S2, S3].
